.TH MDNSD.CONF 5 "October 2026" "mdns 0.1.0" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
mdnsd.conf \- configuration file of the mdns daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR mdns (8)
reads its configuration from
.IR /etc/mdnsd.conf ,
or from the file named on its command line.
The file is read once, at startup.
.PP
The syntax is systemd-style INI:
.IP \(bu 2
Settings are
.I Key=Value
lines in
.I [Section]
blocks.
Section and key names are case-insensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
Lines beginning with
.B #
or
.B ;
are comments; blank lines are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
A value may be wrapped in single or double quotes:
.BR "Name=\(dqIoT to br0\(dq" .
.IP \(bu 2
Repeating a key appends to a list value such as
.BR Interfaces= ;
an empty assignment resets the list.
.IP \(bu 2
Booleans accept
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false ,
.BR on / off
and
.BR 1 / 0 .
.IP \(bu 2
A line ending in a backslash continues on the next line, as in systemd unit
files.
.PP
Syntax errors and unknown settings are reported with the line they are on.
Unknown sections and keys are errors, so that typos fail loudly.
.SH SECTIONS
.SS [Listen]
Optional.
.TP
.B IPv4=
Listen and announce on IPv4 (224.0.0.251).
Default
.BR yes .
.TP
.B IPv6=
Listen and announce on IPv6 (ff02::fb).
Default
.BR yes .
.PP
At least one of the two must be enabled.
The families are independent of each other: each has its own sockets, device
lists and reflectors, and a device reachable over both appears once per family.
.SS [Ipc]
Optional.
.TP
.B Socket=
Path of the control socket used by
.BR mdnsctl (1).
Default
.IR /run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock .
.IP
The directory must exist and be writable by the daemon; the systemd service
creates
.I /run/mdnsd
for it.
Unix socket paths are limited to about 108 bytes.
The socket is created with mode 0660 (owner and group only).
A stale socket left by a crashed run is replaced; a live instance, or a file
that is not a socket, is never overwritten.
.SS [Devices]
Optional.
.TP
.B MinimumLifetime=
The least a device is kept after it was last heard from, as a systemd time span
such as
.BR 5min ,
.B 90s
or
.BR 1h ,
at most one day.
Default
.BR 5min .
A device lives for the longest TTL it announced, but never for less than this, so
a device that announces tiny TTLs is not dropped between two of the daemon's
checks.
.B 0
removes the minimum: each device then lives for exactly its longest TTL.
.IP
It is also the price of a device that vanishes without saying goodbye: it stays
listed, and announced to the other networks, for up to this long.
Keep
.B RefreshBefore=
shorter than it, because that margin is counted back from the end of this
lifetime; the daemon warns at startup if it is not.
.SS [Probe]
Optional.
Both settings are systemd time spans, such as
.BR 30s ,
.B 4min
or
.BR "1h 30min" ,
or a bare number of seconds.
.TP
.B RefreshBefore=
Ask a device to answer again this long before it would expire.
Default
.BR 30s ;
.B 0
or
.B off
turns it off.
A device lives for as long as the longest TTL it announced, and at least
.B MinimumLifetime=
(five minutes by default).
This long before that runs out, the daemon asks that device, and only it, for
the records it announced, and asks again about every quarter of this margin
until it answers or expires.
A speaker whose records live seventy-five minutes is asked at about seventy-four
minutes; a device with short records just before its minimum lifetime is up.
Devices that are heard from regularly are never asked, and a device known only by
the address it was heard from has nothing to ask and is left alone.
It must be at least 30 seconds.
.IP
Because of the minimum lifetime, a TTL shorter than this margin is not a problem:
such a device is not due as soon as it is heard, it lives
.B MinimumLifetime=
after it was last heard and is asked 30 seconds before that.
The cost is that a device that really vanishes stays listed, and announced to the
other networks, for up to that long.
A margin longer than the lifetime makes every device due all the time; it is then
asked once per quarter of the margin, not continuously.
.TP
.B Interval=
Also probe every interface for devices this often: browse for service types and
instances.
Not set by default.
It must be at least 10 seconds.
This is how a new quiet device that never announces itself is found; without it,
new devices are found at startup, when they announce themselves (most do when
they join a network) and by
.BR "mdnsctl probe" .
With
.B RefreshBefore=
off, an
.B Interval=
has to stay under
.B MinimumLifetime=
to keep devices listed, and the daemon warns about it at startup.
.SS [Advertise]
Optional.
.TP
.B Hostname=
A name under
.B .local
that the daemon announces, and answers queries for, with this host's own
addresses on each interface it listens on, for example
.BR router.local .
A bare label such as
.B router
means
.BR router.local .
.IP
Each interface is told the address at which this host can be reached on it:
the IPv4 socket publishes an A record with the interface's primary IPv4
address, and the IPv6 socket an AAAA record with its link-local IPv6 address,
which is what the kernel uses as the source address towards the mDNS group
there.
An interface that has no address of a family is skipped, with a message in
the log.
The addresses are read once, at startup: restart the daemon after changing
them.
.IP
The name is announced and kept alive like a reflected device, answers queries
(a reflected device without services), and is withdrawn with a goodbye on
shutdown.
No conflict detection is done: the name is assumed to be yours alone.
.TP
.B TTL=
How long other devices keep the address of the name, as a time span such as
.B 2min
(the default, as Avahi used for host names).
It is announced again at 80% of this, so by default every 96 seconds; a longer
TTL means less traffic, but a stale address lingers that long when this host's
address changes.
At least 10 seconds and at most 24 hours.
.SS [Segment]
One or more are required.
.TP
.B Name=
Required.
A name that is unique within the file, used by
.BR mdnsctl (1)
to refer to the segment.
Quote it if it contains spaces.
.TP
.B Interfaces=
Required.
The interfaces of the segment, separated by commas and/or whitespace.
Every interface must exist when the daemon starts.
.PP
A segment is a group of interfaces whose devices are reflected between
.I every pair
of them: read it as "these networks may see each other".
.IP \(bu 2
A segment with a single interface only tracks: its devices are listed by
.BR mdnsctl (1)
but nothing is reflected.
.IP \(bu 2
An interface may appear in several segments.
This is how one network can see two others that must not see each other.
Reflection is never transitive, because a device reflected onto an interface is
not reflected again.
.IP \(bu 2
Two segments may not share two or more interfaces, since both would reflect
between that pair; sharing one interface is fine.
.IP \(bu 2
An interface may be listed only once per segment.
.SH EXAMPLE
Three networks: a trusted LAN
.RB ( br0 ),
a media VLAN and an IoT Wi-Fi VLAN.
.B br0
and
.B media
see each other's devices, and so do
.B br0
and
.BR wifi ;
.B media
and
.B wifi
never see each other.
.PP
.RS
.nf
[Listen]
IPv4=yes
IPv6=yes

[Ipc]
Socket=/run/mdnsd/mdnsd.sock

[Devices]
MinimumLifetime=5min

[Probe]
RefreshBefore=30s

[Advertise]
Hostname=router.local
TTL=2min

[Segment]
Name=media_stuff
Interfaces=br0,media

# One interface only: tracked, nothing reflected.
[Segment]
Name="IoT"
Interfaces=wifi

[Segment]
Name="IoT to br0"
Interfaces=br0,wifi
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/mdnsd.conf
Default location.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/mdns/examples/mdnsd.conf
Example configuration, installed with the package.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mdns (8),
.BR mdnsctl (1)
